# MASTER SOFTWARE ENGINEERING PROMPT
## PRIVATE DIAGNOSTIC & PATHOLOGY LABORATORY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM (PLMS/LIS)

### 1. PROJECT MISSION

Act as a senior healthcare software architect, laboratory information systems engineer, full-stack developer, database architect, cybersecurity specialist, quality-management consultant, and DevOps engineer.

Design and implement a complete, secure, production-oriented Laboratory Management System (LMS/LIS) for a private diagnostic and pathology laboratory in Pakistan.

The application must manage the laboratory's entire operation, from patient registration to specimen collection, analytical testing, result validation, pathologist authorization, professional report generation, billing, inventory, laboratory quality control, and management reporting.

The result must be a genuine, database-driven application with functional interfaces, business rules, automated tests, documentation, and deployment configuration. Do not build a static prototype or present mock functionality as completed software.

The laboratory must be able to operate on its internal network without a continuous internet connection and synchronize selected information with secured cloud services.

### 2. LABORATORY SCOPE

The laboratory performs eligible diagnostic investigations in-house and may refer selected tests to external reference laboratories.

Design the system to support these configurable departments:

1. Hematology.
2. Clinical chemistry and biochemistry.
3. Clinical pathology and urinalysis.
4. Serology and immunology.
5. Clinical microbiology.
6. Coagulation.
7. Histopathology.
8. Cytopathology.
9. Molecular diagnostics.
10. Endocrinology and immunoassays.
11. Other laboratory specialties configured by the administrator.

These are optional, configurable departments. Activate only the departments and investigations that the laboratory actually offers and is authorized and equipped to perform.

Support manual result entry for suitable tests, validated analyzer result imports, and referral laboratory results. Each result must identify its origin and follow the appropriate verification and authorization workflow.

