### 4. HYBRID CLOUD AND LOCAL NETWORK ARCHITECTURE

Deploy the primary application and operational database on a secured local laboratory server.

Local components:
- Web frontend.
- Backend API.
- PostgreSQL.
- Local report and document storage.
- Background processing.
- Cloud synchronization worker.
- Local backup and monitoring services.

Cloud components may include:
- Encrypted off-site backups.
- Secure remote access.
- Optional patient and physician portals.
- Central administration for authorized deployments.
- Approved messaging and report-delivery integrations.
- Monitoring and synchronization status.

Use the local database as the authoritative source for local laboratory transactions.

Implement a transactional outbox pattern so that changes requiring cloud synchronization are recorded with the corresponding local business transaction.

The synchronization service must support:
- Authenticated and encrypted connections.
- Stable event identifiers.
- Idempotent processing.
- Retry with appropriate backoff.
- Acknowledgements.
- Failure monitoring.
- Duplicate detection.
- Conflict review.
- Recovery after internet outages.

Never allow uncoordinated cloud and local databases to overwrite clinical or financial records through last-write-wins synchronization.

Keep essential LAN workflows operational during internet outages. Clearly identify delayed cloud synchronization, failed message delivery, and unavailable remote services.

Never report a backup, delivery, synchronization, or restoration operation as successful unless the operation has been verified.

### 5. USER ROLES AND PERMISSIONS

Implement server-enforced role-based access control.

Administrator:
- Manage configuration, accounts, departments, roles, and system settings.
- Review audit records and system health.
- Manage backup and deployment settings.

Receptionist:
- Register patients.
- Create orders.
- Print labels and receipts.
- Access permitted patient information.

Phlebotomist:
- Manage collection worklists.
- Record collection details.
- Document recollection and collection exceptions.

Laboratory technician:
- Access assigned departmental worklists.
- Record analytical results.
- Review permissible analyzer imports and technical information.
- Record repeats, dilutions, and processing comments.

Department supervisor:
- Review technical exceptions.
- Manage assigned worklists.
- Perform authorized technical verification.

Pathologist or authorized result reviewer:
- Review eligible clinical results.
- Record permitted interpretations.
- Authorize and release reports within assigned clinical scope.

Cashier:
- Manage invoices, payments, receipts, refunds, and daily reconciliation.

Inventory manager:
- Manage reagents, consumables, stock lots, suppliers, and purchase orders.

Quality manager:
- Manage QC records, equipment maintenance, nonconformities, and corrective actions.

Auditor:
- Read-only access to authorized audit records.

Optional patient and physician portal users:
- Access only records associated with their verified identity and authorized relationship.

Support granular permissions for viewing, creating, editing, verifying, approving, releasing, exporting, and administering records.

Prevent unauthorized users from changing clinical results, authorizing their own results when independent review is required, modifying released reports, or bypassing financial audit controls.

